          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            In a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+model+of+dependent+type+theory"} categorical model of dependent type theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type"} type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/context"} context
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
            is called
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibrant+type"} fibrant
            if the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projection"} projection
            morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo :
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo .
                  %mi T
                  %mo →
                  %mi Γ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p : \Gamma.T \to \Gamma
            from the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/context+extension"} extended context
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibration"} fibration
          %p When we want to study or use fibrancy within type theory, we may be interested in the following properties, which do not necessarily hold:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                The
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+product+type"} Π-type
                is fibrant if its
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/codomain"} codomain
                is,
            %li
              %p
                The
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibrant+replacement"} fibrant replacement
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad"} monad
                commutes with substitution,
            %li
              %p
                A type in context
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
                is fibrant if and only if its [pullback]] (substitution) along any morphism/cell
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi γ
                      %mo :
                      %mi y
                      %mi W
                      %mo →
                      %mi Γ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gamma : y W \to \Gamma
                from a representable context
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi y
                        %mi W
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y W
          %p
            The first property is just practical. The second property allows the fibrant replacement to be internalized as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad"} monad
            on types, so that fibrancy can be defined internally as being an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+over+a+monad"} algebra for that monad
            %span.newWikiWord
              Hofmann-Streicher universe
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Hofmann-Streicher+universe"} ?
            (
            %a{:href => "#HofmannStreicher"}> Hofmann and Streicher
            ) of fibrant types. It should be straightforward to prove that this is equivalent with the second property, and with the property that the fibrant replacement can be taken cellwise.
          %p
            One way to guarantee that properties 1-2 hold, is by defining fibrations as the right class of an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+weak+factorization+system"} algebraic weak factorization system
            generated by a class of
            %em generating left maps
            whose pullback is again a left map (
            %a{:href => "#Nuyts2020"}> Nuyts 2020
            ). (So a fibration is any morphism that right-lifts any generating left map, and a left map is any morphism that left-lifts any fibration.)
          %p Important notions of fibrancy violate the properties above:
          %ul
            %li Kan fibrancy as used in models of HoTT. The problem is that a filling of a horn or open box takes place above a cell that is not part of that horn or open box.
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Segal+condition"} Segal
              fibrancy as one would like to use in models of directed type theory, for the same reason.
          %p
            For this reason,
            %a{:href => "#BoulierTabareau2021"} Boulier and Tabareau
            introduced contextual Kan fibrancy. A type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            in a context
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo .
                  %mi Θ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma.\Theta
            is contextually Kan fibrant in context
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
            if it has fillers for open boxes whose “open” dimension is
            = succeed "-homogeneous," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
